# The gate's hostile-programs row (TASK-26 Q7; ADR-0268 point 3; TASK-21's
# hostile cases) from inside a room with limits: a program that ignores
# Interrupt is ended by the second Ctrl-C (job_kill always works); every bad
# argument to a kernel call comes back as a typed error; random values in
# every call that can return (INV-0032-2) leave the shell, the room and home
# alive; and removing the room with --kill brings its use to zero. (The POSIX
# half, `t_posix hostile`, is tests/posix/scenarios.script, which runs under
# the shell's broker and `ttyd`; no code from outside the system image runs.)
! panicked
! storage failed
< Type help to begin
< \r\n~> 
> room new hostile --mem 64M --procs 16 +w /t/w\r
< hostile --colour 1 --mem 64M --procs 16 +w /t/w
< \r\n~> 
> room hostile\r
< hostile /t/w> 
> stubborn\r
< stubborn: ignoring Interrupt
> \x03
< ^C
< (Ctrl-C again to kill it)
> \x03
< ^C
< stubborn: killed
< hostile /t/w> 
> badptr\r
< \r\nhostile /t/w> 
> echo $status\r
< \r\n0
< hostile /t/w> 
> fuzz\r
< \r\nhostile /t/w> 
> echo $status\r
< \r\n0
< hostile /t/w> 
> memhog --hold 1\r
< memhog: refused after
< hostile /t/w> 
# Everything outside is as it was: the shell answers, the table is whole, and
# the room's use is the shell's alone.
> top\r
< hostile
< of 64M
< of 16
< hostile /t/w> 
> room --leave\r
> cd /\r
< \r\n/> 
> echo home still works\r
< home still works
< \r\n/> 
> room remove hostile --kill\r
< \r\n/> 
> room\r
< home
< \r\n/> 
> poweroff\r
